\f0\b\fs28 Key findings 
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	1	}1. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Zone 1 has the highest average consumption and Zone 3 the lowest. Zone 3 is the most variable relative to its size.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}2. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 All three zones peak in the evening (around 20:00) and are lowest in the early morning (06:00 to 07:00).\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 3. Zones 1 and 2 are lower on weekends, especially Sundays. Zone 3 shows no weekday/weekend drop.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}4. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Consumption is highest in July and August. Zone 3 is the most seasonal, with a high summer-evening peak\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}5. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Temperature is moderately associated with consumption (Spearman about 0.4), but most of that association disappears aftertax removing the hour-of-day and month effects.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 (Full details can be found in \'93data cleaning and data W5\'94)\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\ls1\ilvl0
